</w:pPr>
<w:hyperlink w:history="1" w:anchor="_sec2">
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">Chapter 1: The Light Comes Back</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:hyperlink>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="ContentsEntry"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:hyperlink w:history="1" w:anchor="_sec3">
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">Chapter 2: Footprints in the Salt</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:hyperlink>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Heading1"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:bookmarkStart w:name="_sec2" w:id="2"/>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">Chapter 1: The Light Comes Back</w:t>
</w:r>
<w:bookmarkEnd w:id="2"/>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">The lamp at Gull Point had been dark for eleven years when Morwenna Tregear saw it burning from her kitchen window.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">She set down the kettle and watched. The beam swung once across the bay, slow and yellow, and then it was gone.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">&quot;You saw it too,&quot; said her brother from the doorway. He had his coat on already.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="SceneBreak"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">* * *</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">The path to the point was slick with rain. Morwenna went first, torch in one hand, the other on the cold iron rail her father had fixed to the rock.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">At the top the door of the lamp room stood open. Someone had swept the floor.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Heading1"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:bookmarkStart w:name="_sec3" w:id="3"/>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">Chapter 2: Footprints in the Salt</w:t>
</w:r>
<w:bookmarkEnd w:id="3"/>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">By morning the whole village had an opinion. Mrs Kitto at the shop said it was boys from Penzance. The vicar said nothing at all, which Morwenna noticed.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">She went back up alone after lunch. The floor was still clean, but a line of salty footprints led from the stairs to the window and stopped.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="SceneBreak"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">* * *</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">Her brother was waiting at the bottom of the path when she came down.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">&quot;Well?&quot; he said.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Normal"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">&quot;Whoever it was,&quot; she said, &quot;they never came back down.&quot;</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Heading1"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:bookmarkStart w:name="_sec4" w:id="4"/>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">About the Author</w:t>
</w:r>
<w:bookmarkEnd w:id="4"/>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">Ada Penrose writes quiet mysteries set on the Cornish coast. She lives in Falmouth with a patient dog and too many tide tables.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="Heading1"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:bookmarkStart w:name="_sec5" w:id="5"/>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">A Small Request</w:t>
</w:r>
<w:bookmarkEnd w:id="5"/>
</w:p>
<w:p>
<w:pPr>
<w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/>
</w:pPr>
<w:r>
<w:t xml:space="preserve">If you enjoyed this story, a short honest review on the store where you bought it helps other readers find it. Thank you for reading.</w:t>
</w:r>
</w:p>
